== Examples of Our Own Time

#emph[Daniel Waldow] Esquire, #emph[citizen] and #emph[Mercer,] who was chosen alderman of #emph[London,] is a further proof of the doctrine laid down before. From my own experience I could say much of his #emph[bounty] and #emph[charity,] and also of the #emph[plentiful estate with which God blessed him for it.] But I will rather give it to you#super[+] in the words of that holy man and blessed servant of Christ in the work of the ministry, Mr. #emph[James Nalton,] now with God. He was more closely acquainted with Mr. #emph[Waldow,] and so all the more fit to preach his funeral sermon and to set out his life for our #emph[imitation.] His words are these: “He was a man eminent and exemplary in the grace of charity, as appeared by his great bounty shown on every occasion. No good man, minister or other, who came to propose any work of charity, public or private, ever needed to do more than propose it. For his heart was so set on works of mercy that he forestalled any pressing request by his Christian and heroic liberality. He made no more of giving ten pounds to a work of charity than many other rich men make of giving ten shillings. His charity had two rare things that went with it, which made it all the more remarkable and worthy of praise.

1\. “He did good while he lived. He carried his lantern before him: he made his own hands his executors and his own eyes his overseers. Some will part with their riches when they can keep them no longer. This is like a cutpurse who, when he is spotted or chased, will drop a purse of gold because he can keep it no longer. But to be doing good in our lifetime, while we have opportunity, is an act of faith, and an evidence that we can trust God with our estate and our children, that he will provide for them when our heads are laid in the grave.

2\. “He gave out his charity so secretly, without any self-seeking or pharisaical vainglory, that his left hand did not know what his right hand was doing. So he would often go with a hundred pounds under his cloak to some godly friends, asking them to distribute it among such honest poor people as stood in most need of relief.”

In short, he did so much good while he lived, as if he meant to have nothing to do when he died; and yet he gave so generously when he came to die, as if he had done no good while he lived.

Many, I know, are apt to say that they have many children, and so cannot give. So had Mr. #emph[Waldow:] he had nine children alive at his death. But providing for them was no hindrance to his charity, and his charity did no harm to his children; rather, it secured a blessing on them.

Mr. #emph[John Walter, citizen] and #emph[Draper] of #emph[London,] was remarkably charitable, not only at his death but in the whole course of his life, even from his younger years. To avoid vainglory, his way was to send considerable sums of money to various poor families by the hands of others, whose faithfulness he could rely on. For this, God not only blessed him with a large estate, but also gave him such contentment in it that he sat down abundantly satisfied. And he made #emph[a solemn vow and promise to God] that he would give the surplus of his estate, whatever might come to him in the future from his calling and business, to charitable uses. That you#super[+] may be assured of the truth of this, I have given you here his own words, copied out of his last will and testament, which are as follows.

I thought fit to declare that about twenty years ago, when the Lord had entrusted me with a suitable estate, sufficient to maintain those in my charge and to provide fit portions for my wife and children after my death, I resolved that whatever further estate the Lord should be pleased to entrust me with, I would bestow it on charitable uses.

After this vow, finding his estate wonderfully increased, he began to build #emph[almshouses:] one in the parish of St. #emph[George] in #emph[Southwark,] another in St. #emph[Mary Newington.] For in those #emph[parishes,] he observed, there were many blind, lame and distressed poor people, and not one #emph[almshouse] in them. He also built a #emph[chapel] near one of his almshouses, for the poor people to serve God in daily. Having built his #emph[almshouses,] he bought #emph[lands] and #emph[houses of inheritance] with the #emph[poor’s stock,] and settled them on the Drapers’ Company, both for the relief and support of his #emph[almspeople] after his death, and for carrying out the other charitable gifts named in his last will and testament. While he lived, he used to go himself once a month to his #emph[almshouses] in his worst clothes (so that he might not be suspected of being their founder), and gave the poor people their promised allowance. No doubt the best charity is that which, #emph[like the Nile,] has its various #emph[streams seen,] but its #emph[fountain hidden.] Having built his #emph[almshouses] and endowed them with a good income, he then relieved #emph[poor people] and #emph[families] with considerable sums of money out of the rest of his #emph[poor’s stock] (which increased daily through God’s blessing on his work and efforts in his calling). And he gave #emph[much bread every week] to the poor of several #emph[outer parishes.] All this he did while he was still living, besides what he gave on the same account at his death, which was also very considerable, as his will further shows. And in spite of all this, he gave and left about ten thousand pounds to his wife and his two daughters. This, without question, is the surest way to have our WILLS carried out: to see them carried out in our own lifetime, since many #emph[executors] prove #emph[executioners] of WILLS.

#emph[William Pennoyer] Esquire, #emph[citizen] and #emph[merchant] of #emph[London,] was a person made wholly of #emph[mercy] and #emph[goodness, bounty] and #emph[liberality,] which he showed in the whole course of his life, from the time he first set up in the world. Many years before his death, he turned a great part of the stock he traded with into lands of inheritance, to the value of #emph[four hundred pounds a year.] Being eminently charitable, he lived as frugally as he could, spending about two hundred pounds a year on himself, his wife and his family; and the rest of his income he gave wholly to charitable uses. So I have been told by those who lived long with him and were closely related to him.

To give you#super[+] a clear demonstration of his Christian charity, and of God’s repaying it to him here in this life with temporal blessings, I will list some of the #emph[legacies] he left to charitable uses in his last will and testament, passing over those he gave to his rich relatives and friends.

To poor ministers, widows, and others in distress, about £150

To four of his poor tenants, £20

Also £800, to be laid out here in woolen cloth or other goods, to be sent to #emph[New England] for the use of his poor relatives there.

He gave lands to the value of twenty pounds #emph[a year] to certain trustees, to pay for the schooling of forty boys.

To #emph[Bristol,] £54 #emph[a year] toward the maintenance of a schoolmaster and lecturer to preach a weekday lecture there; and to other charitable uses.

He also settled £20 #emph[a year] on #emph[trustees] for the schooling of forty poor children in or near #emph[Whitechapel,] and 40 shillings a year to buy Bibles for some of the children.

He gave £12 #emph[a year] for the maintaining of a school at the #emph[Hay] in #emph[Brecknockshire,] and 40 shillings more a year to buy books for the scholars.

Also £10 #emph[a year] for the maintenance of poor distressed people in the #emph[Hospital] of #emph[Bethlehem] in #emph[London.]

And £10 #emph[a year] to ten of the blindest, oldest and poorest clothworkers, at the discretion of the masters, wardens and assistants of that company for the time being.

He gave £40 #emph[a year] to #emph[Christ-church Hospital,] for apprenticing four children a year, and 40 shillings more a year to buy each of the children a Bible.

Besides these, he gave his poor relatives more than two thousand pounds by his will.

And by a codicil attached to it, he left to certain #emph[trustees] a #emph[thousand pounds] to be given to honest poor people, and also £300 for releasing poor prisoners. These sums have been paid into the hands of the #emph[trustees] by Mr. #emph[Richard Loton] and #emph[Michael Davison] Esq., who to their honor proved themselves #emph[faithful executors] of this charitable will.

#emph[Thomas Arnold, citizen] and #emph[Haberdasher] of #emph[London:] when he first set up for himself, his stock was not great. But being charitably disposed and ready for every good work, he saw his estate, through God’s blessing, increase very much.

His charity in his lifetime appeared not only in his eagerness to share with those he saw in want, for their relief, but also in his frequent inquiries of others after poor people who were overburdened with children or otherwise distressed. Indeed, he paid men to make it their business to find honest poor people on whom he might bestow his charity. He also entrusted others with considerable sums of money to distribute among the poorest, charging them to have special regard to the honest poor, those whom they judged truly feared God. That he was no #emph[loser] but a #emph[gainer] by his #emph[liberality] is plain from this: God so blessed him in his calling that he attained to an #emph[alderman]’s estate, and was chosen to that #emph[office.] More than that, he gave up his calling in the city and withdrew to the country, so that he might better mind God and the concerns of his soul more, and the world and its concerns less.

#emph[John Clark, doctor] of #emph[medicine,] was a man of great repute for his #emph[learning, piety] and #emph[charity,] and for a while #emph[President] of the #emph[College] of #emph[Physicians.] His custom was to lay aside all his #emph[Lord’s Day] fees as a #emph[sacred stock] for charitable uses. What he received on #emph[God’s day] he devoted entirely to God, counting it a kind of #emph[sacrilege] to take it for himself or for any common use. For this the Lord was pleased so to prosper him in his calling that, though at first his practice was small and his estate not very great, afterward his practice so increased, and the world so flowed in on him, that he lived #emph[plentifully] and #emph[comfortably,] and gave his children generous portions.

The same was also practiced by #emph[John Bathurst, doctor] of #emph[medicine,] whom I knew very well. His #emph[Lord’s Day] fees were always kept as #emph[a bank for the poor,] wholly devoted to their use and spent on it. This was so far from lessening his income that, by the blessing of God on his practice, it was greatly increased by it in a few years. For when he first came to #emph[London,] he brought little estate with him and had few acquaintances here, #emph[Yorkshire] being his native county, where he had spent his former days. Yet the Lord was pleased so to prosper him in his calling that in twenty years he bought #emph[lands of inheritance] to the value of a #emph[thousand pounds a year.] I say what I know to be certain; for in the opinion of some, his estate at his death was worth no less than two thousand pounds a year.

Dr. #emph[Edmond Trench] also followed the same course, as his wife and various other friends of his testify. And it is certain that this was no #emph[damage] but a great #emph[advantage] to him; for he had as many patients as his weak body would allow him to visit. And though he lived in a full and plentiful way, often and cheerfully entertaining ministers and scholars at his table, yet he gained a very considerable estate, which he left to his wife and children; and in them not only his memory but his piety still lives on.

I have good grounds to believe that many other #emph[physicians] make conscience of this duty. But oh, that all would do the same! They would certainly be no #emph[losers] by it at the year’s end, but would find God’s blessing on their #emph[calling] and #emph[estates,] prospering them in both.

And here a #emph[case of conscience] comes to my mind that is worth inquiring into, namely, #emph[whether physicians may lawfully take their Lord’s Day fees for their own private use.] I mean the fees they receive from their #emph[patients] on the #emph[Sabbath day.]

I do not deny that #emph[works of mercy] may and ought to be done on that day. For the Lord says, “I desire mercy, not sacrifice” (Matt 9:13), that is, mercy #emph[rather] than sacrifice. Nor do I deny that physicians may receive their fees for their #emph[pains,] from the richer sort especially. But I very much question whether they may #emph[take those fees for themselves.] For the Lord has given us six days out of seven, in which we may and ought to follow our callings for our own livelihood and that of those who belong to us; but he has sanctified and set apart the Christian Sabbath for his own honor and service. So it seems only reasonable that what comes to us on that day should be set apart and used for his more immediate use and service. But I will not impose this as a necessary duty, at least not on all, since circumstances may differ. For now I leave it to be settled by the consciences of those who are most concerned in it.

#emph[Samuel Dunche] of #emph[Pusey,] in the #emph[County] of #emph[Berks,] Esquire, was a person who, according to the #emph[apostle]’s rule, #emph[did good to all, but especially to those of the household of faith.] His custom was to send money every year to several towns for the relief of their poor, such as #emph[Stow] upon the Woolds in #emph[Gloucestershire,] #emph[Norliche,] #emph[Lamburn,] and others not mentioned here. And on the last named he settled lands of inheritance forever for the same use. And to #emph[Rumsey] in #emph[Hampshire] he gave by deed, on the same account, a lease of 99 years, to begin after his death. The poor of that town also, whom he called his #emph[almspeople,] had #emph[weekly relief from him] during his life, and many other towns along with them had a large share in the same bounty.

He sent several poor children of that town to school, and also some of those belonging to #emph[Faringdon,] and he not only paid for their schooling but also supplied them with all such books as were fit and suitable for them. He also had several good books printed at his own expense, which he gave freely to the poor, so that they might be the more encouraged to read and to acquaint themselves with the concerns of another and better life.

He further gave considerable sums of money every year for the constant supply of such godly ministers as he knew to be in want. And on several of them he settled considerable annuities, £10 and £20 #emph[a year] for their lives, besides the legacies, not small ones, that he gave to some of them at his death.

Besides all this, his hand was always open and ready to give whenever any fit occasion was offered him. Indeed, such was the largeness of his heart and the tenderness of his compassion that he could hardly pass by anyone he judged a proper object of his charity without contributing freely and bountifully to that person’s relief.

So this pious gentleman honored God with his wealth, and ventured on the words of the royal #emph[prophet,] #emph[to cast his bread upon the waters.] The unbelieving world counts this mere folly and usually reckons it among its losses. Yet he, to his advantage, according to the promise attached to it, #emph[found it again, and not after many days.] This bread, like the loaves with which Christ fed the multitude, was multiplied in his hands, and his oil increased by pouring out. He was only a younger brother, and the estate settled on him was only £800 #emph[a year,] or thereabouts. And yet, in spite of this (I had almost said) excess of charity, his estate was so far from being ruined, or in the least diminished, that not only the same bare measure he had received, but a #emph[much] greater one, #emph[pressed down and running over, was measured out to him and his descendants.] So remarkably did God reward his charity in this life, wisely ordering by his good providence that, one way or another, large handfuls were thrown into his bushel from various hands, as overmeasure. There is now left to his heirs an estate of more than double the value of what he received from his father, besides the portions he gave to all his #emph[daughters,] five in number, which were very considerable, to some of them more than £2,000.

And here I hope I may, without offense or vainglory, take the liberty to mention, among others, the #emph[charity] and #emph[liberality] of my dear and honored father, Dr. #emph[William Gouge,] late #emph[pastor] of #emph[Blackfriars, London.] He was eminent in other graces, and so also in that of charity. It was from him that I first heard that #emph[the tenth part of a rich man’s estate is a fit proportion to be devoted and dedicated to God for charitable uses.] But though he commended that to others, yet by what I find in some papers written in his own hand, I may truly say that he gave #emph[the seventh part of all his yearly income] toward maintaining poor scholars at the university and relieving poor families and distressed persons. And how wonderfully God blessed both his #emph[ministry] and his #emph[outward estate] is so well known to all who lived in his days that I suppose it needless for me to say anything about it. Only I may truly apply to him the words of the #emph[psalmist:] “He is ever generous and quick to lend, and his children are blessed.”

Many more instances of the same kind might be added here. Some are of persons who are now with joy reaping in the other world the blessed fruit of the seed they sowed so plentifully in this one. Others are of persons still living among us, some of whom have acknowledged to me #emph[that God has already rewarded them a hundredfold for what they have lent to him by giving it to his poor.] But those already mentioned may be abundantly sufficient to show the truth in hand, and to encourage and stir us up, in imitation of those worthies, to a more ready and conscientious practice of this great duty. It is so essential to Christianity, so well pleasing to God, so creditable to our profession of faith, and so beneficial, not only for our eternal happiness but for our present gain, comfort and prosperity here on earth.
